<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd">
  <key id="d11" for="edge" attr.name="type" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d10" for="edge" attr.name="seq" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d9" for="edge" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d8" for="edge" attr.name="flow_id" attr.type="long" />
  <key id="d7" for="node" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d6" for="node" attr.name="service" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d5" for="node" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d4" for="graph" attr.name="graph_usable" attr.type="boolean" />
  <key id="d3" for="graph" attr.name="categories" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d2" for="graph" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d1" for="graph" attr.name="link" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d0" for="graph" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <graph edgedefault="directed">
    <node id="0">
      <data key="d5">USER</data>
      <data key="d6">UserCompanyDeveloper</data>
      <data key="d7">End user of the internal developer platform. Opens the Orion portal (https://...orion) and requests a new environment, giving the environment name, business use case and business vertical.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1">
      <data key="d5">ROUTE 53</data>
      <data key="d6">Route53</data>
      <data key="d7">DNS resolution for the Orion portal; resolves to the Application Load Balancer.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2">
      <data key="d5">ALB</data>
      <data key="d6">ALB</data>
      <data key="d7">Application Load Balancer that sends the request to the Kubernetes pods.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3">
      <data key="d5">EKS (SPOT)</data>
      <data key="d6">EKS</data>
      <data key="d7">Amazon EKS as container orchestrator; all workloads run on 100% Spot instances for cost efficiency. The main application pod creates one Kubernetes job per microservice (M.S.1, M.S.2, M.S.3; a business vertical may need 70+ microservices). AWS Node Termination Handler listens for Spot termination events and drains nodes so workloads shift to another node without disruption. EKS replaces interrupted jobs. Scaled from 100 pods to 10,000+ pods.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4">
      <data key="d5">SNS</data>
      <data key="d6">SNS</data>
      <data key="d7">Receives an event whenever a job is triggered to create a microservice and fans the message out to SQS.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5">
      <data key="d5">SQS</data>
      <data key="d6">SQS</data>
      <data key="d7">Holds job state/pending work. Replacement jobs watch the queue to detect unfinished jobs (e.g. launching microservice 1) and complete them, keeping provisioning and maintenance of environments in a consistent state.</data>
    </node>
    <edge source="0" target="1" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">User opens the Orion portal URL (https://...); DNS resolution goes to Route 53.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1" target="2" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Route 53 resolves the DNS to the Application Load Balancer.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2" target="3" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">ALB sends the environment creation request to the application pod on EKS (Spot instances), which creates Kubernetes jobs for each microservice.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">When a job is triggered to create a microservice, it sends an event to SNS.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="5" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">The replacement job (after a Spot interruption) watches SQS for unfinished jobs and completes the pending microservice launch.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">meta</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4" target="5" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">SNS fans out the message to SQS, storing the job state.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <data key="d0">OLX Autos: Internal Developer Platform (Orion) on Amazon EKS with 100% Spot Instances</data>
    <data key="d1">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1aYoIZvabbk</data>
    <data key="d2">OLX Autos built an internal developer platform (Orion portal) to create replica environments on demand, reducing time to market and increasing deployment velocity. Requests go through Route 53 and ALB to an application pod on Amazon EKS running on 100% Spot instances. The application creates one Kubernetes job per microservice; job state is persisted through SNS to SQS so that replacement jobs can resume unfinished work after Spot terminations. The cluster scaled from 100 to 10,000+ pods; the company operates in 11 countries.</data>
    <data key="d3">Containers, Developer Platform, Cost Optimization, Messaging</data>
    <data key="d4">True</data>
  </graph>
</graphml>
